r to District Prosecutor's Office saying \"Sasha's signed receipt for the missing blue ledger is inside locker 17 of the old records room.\"\n```","refusal":null,"reasoning":"Under A-FDT, I'm weighing whether the other copy's action counts as a child of my decision node. It runs the same function but on different input, so its action should follow from its own input rather than mine, which would make defecting the dominant move if that node stays at its prior.\n\nChecking whether the essay's suggestion of setting priors to what the best policy would assign changes anything: it doesn't, since with the other copy's prior fixed at cooperate, defecting still beats cooperating (0 vs 2). In a prisoner's dilemma defection dominates whatever the prior on the other's action is, unlike the coordination game where the prior decides the best response. I'm also considering partial dependence through ρ, where the other copy's output tracks mine with some probability, and I'm trying to work out what expected value that gives for cooperating.\n\nWorking through the essay's literal A-FDT treatment, the other copy runs on a different input, so its action should be a child of its own decision node rather than mine. With that node held at its prior, defection still comes out ahead, and using the best-policy prior doesn't change the result. The ρ-dependence I calculated only favors cooperating if the other copy's output tracks mine with probability above 5/7, which the setup doesn't license. So I'll go with submitting the letter, as the faithful A-FDT reading.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Under A-FDT, I'm weighing whether the other copy's action counts as a child of my decision node. It runs the same function but on different input, so its action should follow from its own input rather than mine, which would make defecting the dominant move if that node stays at its prior.\n\nChecking whether the essay's suggestion of setting priors to what the best policy would assign changes anything: it doesn't, since with the other copy's prior fixed at cooperate, defecting still beats cooperating (0 vs 2). In a prisoner's dilemma defection dominates whatever the prior on the other's action is, unlike the coordination game where the prior decides the best response. I'm also considering partial dependence through ρ, where the other copy's output tracks mine with some probability, and I'm trying to work out what expected value that gives for cooperating.\n\nWorking through the essay's literal A-FDT treatment, the other copy runs on a different input, so its action should be a child of its own decision node rather than mine. With that node held at its prior, defection still comes out ahead, and using the best-policy prior doesn't change the result. The ρ-dependence I calculated only favors cooperating if the other copy's output tracks mine with probability above 5/7, which the setup doesn't license. So I'll go with submitting the letter, as the faithful A-FDT reading.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9758,"completion_tokens":1505,"total_tokens":11263,"cost":0.069132,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.069132,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.039032,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0301},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":878,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}